## Google Ads — 30-Day Spend Trend (source: ad platform fixture, data complete through 2026-10-30)

**30-day window (2026-10-01 → 2026-10-30):** spend **$25,421.90**, conversions **131**, CPA **$194.06**.

**Week-over-week (2026-10-17→23 vs 2026-10-24→30):**

| Metric | Prior week | Last week | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| Spend | $5,377.43 | $8,029.35 | **+$2,651.92 (+49.3%)** |
| Conversions | 28 | 42 | **+14 (+50.0%)** |
| CPA | $192.05 | $191.18 | −$0.87 (flat) |

**Shape of the 30-day trend:** The last two weeks ($8,029.35 + $5,377.43 = $13,406.78) account for ~52.7% of the 30-day total, so spend ramped in the back half of the month — roughly ~$6,700/wk recently vs ~$5,200/wk implied for the earlier portion.

**Likely driver:** Spend (+49.3%) and conversions (+50.0%) moved in near lockstep with CPA flat — this is a *delivery/volume* increase, not an efficiency shift. Most consistent with a budget or pacing increase (or seasonal auction expansion) around mid-October. I can't see budget-level or campaign-level detail from this tool, so **confirm what changed around 10/17–10/24 before treating the ramp as intentional** — a change without a verified driver is a question to investigate, not a fact.

**Caveats:**
- Single week-to-week comparison; no weekly decomposition for weeks 1–2 of the window, so the "ramp" is inferred from the two-week share, not observed weekly.
- Conversion volumes (28–42/wk) are moderate; CPA is stable here, but swings of ±$10–15 at this volume would still be within noise.
- Conversions can accrue late on the platform side even with data complete through 2026-10-30; last week's 42 may tick up slightly.
- Media metrics above are from the ad platform, per source-of-truth policy — do not cross-check them against warehouse pipeline data.

